import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>7.3a — Searching &amp; Sorting: Finding Needles, Organizing Haystacks</h1>

<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Discovery Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Your inventory can hold items. But finding a specific item by scanning
        the whole list every time is slow. And displaying items in a useful order
        (by value, by rarity) requires sorting. These are the two most common
        operations on collections and the AP exam tests them relentlessly.
    </p>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Experiment: Linear vs Binary Search</h3>
        <p>Create <code>SearchComparison.java</code>:</p>
        <pre><code>ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt; nums = new ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;();
for (int i = 0; i < 20; i++) nums.add(i * 5);
// nums = [0, 5, 10, 15, ... 95]  — already sorted!

// Linear search: check every element in order
int target = 65;
int steps = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
    steps++;
    if (nums.get(i) == target) {
        System.out.println("Linear found at " + i + " in " + steps + " steps");
        break;
    }
}</code></pre>
        <p>Now implement binary search. Read the <a href="../docs/unit-07/searching-sorting.html">
        searching & sorting docs</a> for the algorithm. Compare step counts for
        target values at the beginning, middle, and end of the list. Which search wins and when?</p>
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Binary Search Requires Sorted Data:</strong> If the list isn't sorted,
        binary search can return wrong answers — not just be slow. The AP exam often
        asks "what does binary search return on this unsorted array?" The answer
        is usually wrong. Read the <a href="../docs/unit-07/searching-sorting.html">docs
        Bug Hunt</a> for the most common binary search mistakes.
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Build: Inventory Sorter</h3>
        <p>Using your Item class and ArrayList:</p>
        <ul>
            <li>Write <code>sortByValue(ArrayList&lt;Item&gt; items)</code> — selection sort
            by item value (lowest first). Swap entire Item objects.</li>
            <li>Write <code>sortByRarity(ArrayList&lt;Item&gt; items)</code> — selection sort
            by rarity (Common < Uncommon < Rare < Epic). Map rarities to numbers first.</li>
            <li>Write <code>binarySearch(ArrayList&lt;Item&gt; sorted, String name)</code> —
            assumes sorted by name. Returns index or -1.</li>
            <li>Test: create 8 items, sort by value, display. Sort by rarity, display.
            Search for an existing and non-existing item name.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Selection sort finds the smallest remaining element and swaps it to
            the front. On a list of N elements, how many comparisons does it make?
            How many swaps?</li>
            <li>Binary search splits the search space in half each step. If you have
            1,000,000 items, how many steps max? (Hint: log₂)</li>
            <li>Your insert method from Unit 6 took O(N) time. ArrayList.add(index, elem)
            also takes O(N) — why? What's happening under the hood?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Implement linear search and binary search</li>
            <li>Implement selection sort on an ArrayList of objects</li>
            <li>Explain when binary search is safe to use</li>
            <li>Compare O(N) vs O(log N) at an intuitive level</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-07/searching-sorting.html">Searching &amp; Sorting</a></li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-07/arraylist-basics.html">ArrayList Basics</a></li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
